### What is the problem?

Given two strings, find the length of the longest substring that appears in both. A substring must be contiguous. For "SALARYPAY" and "MYSALARY", the longest common substring is "SALARY" with length 6.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class LongestCommonSubstring {
    public static int longestCommonSubstring(String a, String b) {
        int m = a.length(), n = b.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];
        int best = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i - 1][j - 1];
                    best = Math.max(best, dp[i][j]);
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = 0;
                }
            }
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

### Why does this matter in banking?

During reconciliation at the National Bank of India, Rajesh compares transaction narration strings from two different systems. A long shared contiguous phrase strongly suggests both narrations were generated by the same automated upstream system, helping identify the source of discrepancies.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.dsa.LongestCommonSubstring;

public class NarrationMatcher {
    public void compare(String narration1, String narration2) {
        int len = LongestCommonSubstring.longestCommonSubstring(narration1, narration2);
        System.out.println("Narration 1: " + narration1);
        System.out.println("Narration 2: " + narration2);
        System.out.println("Longest shared phrase length: " + len);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new NarrationMatcher().compare("SALARYPAY", "MYSALARY");
    }
}
```

```text
Narration 1: SALARYPAY
Narration 2: MYSALARY
Longest shared phrase length: 6
```

### How does the recurrence work?

The table entry dp[i][j] represents the length of the longest common substring that ends exactly at position i of string A and position j of string B. When those two characters match, the run is one longer than the diagonal cell: `dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i-1][j-1]`. When they differ, no common substring can end here, so it resets to 0.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class RecurrenceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String a = "AB", b = "XAB";
        int m = a.length(), n = b.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];
        int best = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i - 1][j - 1];
                    best = Math.max(best, dp[i][j]);
                }
                System.out.println("dp[" + i + "][" + j + "]=" + dp[i][j]
                    + " (a='" + a.charAt(i-1) + "' b='" + b.charAt(j-1) + "')");
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Best: " + best);
    }
}
```

```text
dp[1][1]=0 (a='A' b='X')
dp[1][2]=1 (a='A' b='A')
dp[1][3]=0 (a='A' b='B')
dp[2][1]=0 (a='B' b='X')
dp[2][2]=0 (a='B' b='A')
dp[2][3]=2 (a='B' b='B')
Best: 2
```

### Why is the answer the table maximum, not a corner?

Unlike the longest common subsequence where the answer is in dp[m][n], the longest common substring can end at any pair of positions. So the answer is the maximum value found anywhere in the table, which you track with a running variable during the fill.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class MaxAnywhereDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // The longest common substring might end in the middle, not at the end
        System.out.println(LongestCommonSubstring.longestCommonSubstring("XABCY", "ZABCW")); // 3 ("ABC")
        // Answer is 3, found at dp[4][4], not at dp[5][5]
    }
}
```

```text
3
```

### How is this different from the Longest Common Subsequence?

| Aspect | Common Substring | Common Subsequence |
|---|---|---|
| Contiguity | Required | Not required |
| On mismatch | Reset to 0 | Keep max(top, left) |
| Answer location | Maximum in table | Corner dp[m][n] |
| Use case | Exact shared phrase | Ordered similarity |

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class SubstringVsSubsequence {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String a = "ABCXYZ", b = "XYZABC";
        System.out.println("Common Substring:    " + LongestCommonSubstring.longestCommonSubstring(a, b)); // 3
        System.out.println("Common Subsequence:  " + LongestCommonSubsequence.lcs(a, b));                 // 3
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

If either string is empty, the result is 0. If both strings are identical, the result is their length. If they share no common character, the result is 0.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class EdgeCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(LongestCommonSubstring.longestCommonSubstring("", "ABC"));    // 0
        System.out.println(LongestCommonSubstring.longestCommonSubstring("ABC", "ABC")); // 3
        System.out.println(LongestCommonSubstring.longestCommonSubstring("ABC", "XYZ")); // 0
    }
}
```

```text
0
3
0
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

Time is O(m*n) where m and n are the lengths of the two strings. Space is O(m*n) for the table, reducible to O(min(m,n)) by using only two rolling rows since each row depends only on the previous row.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How would you return the actual substring, not just the length?
- How would you find all common substrings of a given minimum length?
- Can this be solved with a suffix array in O((m+n) log(m+n)) time?
- How does this relate to the edit distance problem?
